MA_FINDINGS = "MOROCCO (MA): findings for cl-stack-calendars#44\n\nLIST (public administrations, décret n° 2-77-169 du 28 février 1977 as amended). The current official table is on the ministry page https://mmsp.gov.ma/fr/nos-metiers/horaires-de-travail-et-jours-f%C3%A9ri%C3%A9s (cached ma/mmsp-jours-feries-fr.html). It lists 17 days: 1 Moharram (1 day), Aïd Al Mawlid on 12 Rabii I (2 days), Aïd Al Fitr on 1 Chawal (2 days), Aïd Al Adha on 10 Dhu al-hijja (2 days), 1 Jan, 11 Jan, 14 Jan (from 2024), 1 May, 30 Jul, 14 Aug, 20 Aug, 21 Aug, 6 Nov and 18 Nov.\n- Two days for each Eid and for the Mawlid: the MMSP table says so. Medias24 (2014-01-10, secondary) quotes décret 2-00-166 (10 May 2000), which says the religious feasts give 2 days off in the administrations. The private sector uses a different list, décret n° 2-04-426 (paid holidays), which these data do not model. I found no source showing any year 2003–2026 with a 1-day Mawlid in the administrations. timeanddate shows the second Mawlid day consistently only from 2020, which I take to be a gap in its data.\n- Yennayer (Nouvel an amazigh, 14 January): the Royal Cabinet communiqué of 3 May 2023, as cited by MMSP, followed by décret n° 2-23-688 du 5 décembre 2023, published in BO (Arabic edition) n° 7256 of 14 Dec 2023. Text: https://mmsp.gov.ma/sites/default/files/publications/Decret_N_2-23-688_20122023.pdf (cached). The first holiday was 14 Jan 2024. python-holidays agrees (from 2024).\n- Fête de l'Unité (31 October): a Royal Office statement of 4 Nov 2025 (MAP via maroc.ma, https://www.maroc.ma/en/news/his-majesty-king-proclaims-october-31-unity-day-statement-royal-office, cached) proclaimed it, after UNSC resolution 2797/2025. It was implemented by décret n° 2-25-1140 of 27 Jan 2026, published in BO n° 7480 of 5 Feb 2026, p. 950. Those BO details come from a search snippet of the Adala consolidated text; I could not read it directly. The first occurrence is 31 Oct 2026. python-holidays agrees (from 2026). Since the statement came out on 4 Nov 2025, 31 Oct 2025 was not a holiday.\n- Fête du Trône 30 July / Fête de la Jeunesse 21 August: under Hassan II these were 3 March and 9 July. Décret n° 2-00-166 du 10 mai 2000 changed the list (number and date from secondary citations; the BO text was not found). Mohammed VI's accession was 23 July 1999 and his enthronement and first Throne speech 30 July 1999. Because the decree of May 2000 came before July 2000, I list 30 July and 21 August from 2000. python-holidays instead uses 3 March and 9 July for 2000 and 30 July / 21 August only from 2001. That disagreement is open: no 2000 source either way. I list neither 3 March 2000 nor 9 July 2000.\n- 20 August, 14 August, 6 November, 18 November and 11 January: all on the list for the whole 2000–2027 window.\n\nDECLARED ISLAMIC DATES. Authority: Ministère des Habous et des Affaires islamiques (moon-sighting communiqués). habous.gov.ma is in maintenance (only prayer times are online), mapexpress.ma returns 403, sgg.gov.ma and adala.justice.gov.ma refuse connections, and the Wayback Machine was intermittently offline. So no year reaches 'primary': every Islamic date is a Habous communiqué relayed by the press (Hespress, Le360, TelQuel, Le Matin, SNRT) or the ICOP list of each country's official first day of the month (https://astronomycenter.net/icop/{shw,hej,muh,raa}NN.html, NN = Hijri year − 1400, all cached under ~/.cache/cl-stack-calendars/icop/). For a few Mawlid / Muharram dates (Mawlid 2003, 2004, 2009, 2010, 2013; Muharram 2005, 2012, 2014) only timeanddate was available.\n- Years in MA_YEARS: 2003–2026, all 'secondary'.\n- Missing: 2000–2002 (ICOP has no Morocco entries for 1420–1422 and timeanddate is empty) and 2027 (nothing announced yet). For these, use MA_ISLAMIC rules and expect ±1 day errors.\n- timeanddate vs communiqué/ICOP, where the communiqué wins: Fitr 2012 (20 Aug, not 19), Fitr 2013 (9 Aug, not 8), Fitr 2016 (6 Jul, not 7), Adha 2013 (16 Oct, not 15), Adha 2015 (24 Sep, not 23), Adha 2016 (12 Sep, not 13), Adha 2017 (1 Sep, not 2), Muharram 2010 (7 Dec, not 8), Muharram 2019 (1 Sep, not 31 Aug). Do not use timeanddate for Morocco's Islamic dates.\n- Open conflicts kept at the ICOP date: Adha 2004 (1 Feb vs 2 Feb), Adha 2006 (11 Jan vs 10), Muharram 2007 (21 Jan vs 20), Mawlid 2007 (1 Apr vs 31 Mar), Fitr 2008 (1 Oct vs 2 Oct), Adha 2007 (21 Dec vs 20).\n- Extra days off: none confirmed. timeanddate's 'Government Holiday' rows (2014-07-28, 2019-06-07, 2023-04-24, 2025-06-08/09, 2026-03-23, 2026-05-29) are in MA_ADHOC as unconfirmed leads. 2025 Adha: the King asked people not to perform the sacrifice, but the holiday was kept.\n\npython-holidays 0.109 (secondary) marks every MA Islamic date as estimated, so it cannot settle any of these dates. Its fixed list matches the decree except for the 2000 transition noted above.\n"
